File submissions/ruby-async-cicloid/app.rb (Store) and server.rb. In Store#initialize add `require "async/condition"` at the top of the file, `@wake = Async::Condition.new`, and prepared transaction statements `@begin = @db.prepare("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")`, `@commit = @db.prepare("COMMIT")`, `@rollback = @db.prepare("ROLLBACK")`, `@passive = @db.prepare("PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(PASSIVE)")`. Replace begin_batch with:

    def begin_batch
      return @batch if @batch
      @begin.execute
      @batch = Async::Promise.new
      @wake.signal # the committer resumes on the next reactor iteration, after every worker already ready in this pass
      @batch
    end

    def run_committer(task)
      task.async { loop { @wake.wait; commit(@batch) } }
    end

`commit(batch)` keeps its body but uses `@commit.execute` / `@rollback.execute` and `checkpoint` uses `@passive.execute`. In server.rb, inside the `Async do |task|` block, call `store.run_committer(task)` before `server.run(task)`. Do not add a `task.yield` in the committer loop: Async 2.46's Condition#signal pushes the waiter through a Thread::Queue, which schedules it for the next loop through the reactor, so the requests ready in the current pass already join the batch exactly as the current `task.yield` achieves (an extra yield would delay each commit by one more pass and add ~0.5 ms latency).
